---
// src/pages/attitude.astro  →  arizvi.com/attitude
// Reached only by clicking the mark in the nav. Not linked from anywhere else.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Fill these in. Each becomes one row in the Flying section.
const videos = [
  { title: 'Sunset landing', note: 'Landing at Friday Harbor into a sunset. The video is sped up 4X.', url: '/media/flying/sunset-landing.mp4' },
  { title: 'Water landing', note: 'Water landing in Winter Haven, FL. Sea planes are some of the most fun vehicles in the world!', url: '/media/flying/water-landing.mp4' },
];
const channel = ''; // YouTube channel URL; the "All of the videos" link appears once this is set

const HEX = 'M92 50 L71 86.4 L29 86.4 L8 50 L29 13.6 L71 13.6 Z';
---

<Base title="The curious are rewarded" description="What the mark on this site is, and why." noindex>
  <div class="attitude">

    <section class="hero">
      <!-- Same file as public/mark-animated-loop.svg, inlined so it can respect reduced motion. -->
      <svg class="instrument" viewBox="0 0 100 100" role="img" aria-labelledby="mark-title">
        <title id="mark-title">Hexagonal attitude indicator taking off and turning left</title>
        <defs><clipPath id="hx"><path d={HEX} /></clipPath></defs>
        <g clip-path="url(#hx)">
          <g class="ball">
            <animateTransform attributeName="transform" type="rotate" values="0 50 50;0 50 50;0 50 50;22 50 50;22 50 50;0 50 50;0 50 50;0 50 50" keyTimes="0;.12;.28;.44;.6;.72;.86;1" calcMode="spline" keySplines="0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1" dur="9s" repeatCount="indefinite" />
            <animateTransform attributeName="transform" type="translate" additive="sum" values="0 0;0 0;0 18;0 18;0 18;0 18;0 0;0 0" keyTimes="0;.12;.28;.44;.6;.72;.86;1" calcMode="spline" keySplines="0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1" dur="9s" repeatCount="indefinite" />
            <rect x="-80" y="-130" width="260" height="180" fill="#fff" />
            <rect x="-80" y="50" width="260" height="180" fill="#000" />
            <path d="M38 38 H62 M42 26 H58" stroke="#000" stroke-width="2" />
            <path d="M44 44 H56 M44 32 H56" stroke="#000" stroke-width="1.2" />
            <path d="M38 62 H62 M42 74 H58" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" />
            <path d="M44 56 H56 M44 68 H56" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.2" />
          </g>
        </g>
        <path d="M50 15.6 L46 22.6 H54 Z" fill="#000" />
        <g fill="#000" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" paint-order="stroke">
          <rect x="27" y="47" width="15" height="6" /><rect x="58" y="47" width="15" height="6" /><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="3.5" />
        </g>
        <path d={HEX} fill="none" stroke="#000" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="miter" />
      </svg>
      <h1>The curious are rewarded</h1>
      <p>The mark on this site is an attitude indicator. I'm a pilot, and it's the instrument I look at most. Here's what's going on in it, and why each part is there. The link at the end of each section takes you to the research it points at.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>It shows an attitude</h2>
      <p>An attitude indicator draws the horizon the way the aircraft sees it: sky on top, ground below, and a fixed little aircraft symbol in the middle. Nose up -> horizon drops. Bank -> horizon tilts. It sits in the middle of every panel because it's the one you check most. The mark is frozen at 15 degrees nose-up, wings level, which is what it shows in the first few seconds after takeoff.</p>
      <p>It's also the oldest answer to the question my thesis asks. Most interfaces assume the room holds still. A cockpit never does, so the instrument moves the world and keeps you fixed. My PhD is basically about that inversion: what happens to technology when the room moves (a van), shrinks (a small apartment), or isn't really a room at all (a card table).</p>
      <p class="label crumb"><a href="/work/phd/">Interaction systems for constrained spaces →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>It's always day zero</h2>
      <p>Pilots call the moment the wheels leave the ground "rotation". At that point nothing is proven yet, you have the least altitude you'll have all flight, and the instrument shows you exactly what you're doing. I like that as a way to work. Every project on this site started at rotation: a question, a field site, and an artefact that didn't exist yet. Proteus was a lab prototype before it was a company. Bodhika was a study with 9 parents before it was a lab with a school in it. The climb is the part with the most decisions and the least margin, and it's the part I actually enjoy. Day zero, every day.</p>
      <p class="label crumb"><a href="/projects/">The projects, at their current altitude →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>It's a hexagon because of a chart</h2>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M84 50 L67 79.5 L33 79.5 L16 50 L33 20.5 L67 20.5 Z" fill="none" stroke="#000" stroke-width="4" />
          <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="5" fill="#000" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>A VOR, as drawn on an aeronautical chart.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Aircraft instruments are round, so why a hexagon? On aeronautical charts, a VOR (the ground beacon that aircraft navigate by) is drawn as a hexagon with a dot in the middle. You fly from one VOR to the next. Each one only tells you where the next one is, and that's been enough to cross continents for 70 years.</p>
      <p>The research has gone the same way: a van -> small homes -> a card table -> a classroom in Hyderabad. I didn't plan that route. Each site was just the next beacon in range from the last one. The About page says "where good problems take me" for the same reason.</p>
      <p class="label crumb"><a href="/work/#paths">Where the research went →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Two bars and a dot</h2>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="16" y="44" width="24" height="12" fill="#000" />
          <rect x="60" y="44" width="24" height="12" fill="#000" />
          <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="6" fill="#000" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>The aircraft symbol. Also a Fitts's law task.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>On most attitude indicators the aircraft symbol is two short bars and a dot. Those are also the three shapes in the oldest experiment in HCI: two targets and a pointer moving between them. Paul Fitts ran it in 1954. The result (time to reach a target depends on how far away it is and how big it is) still decides how big the buttons on your phone are.</p>
      <p>Fitts kept the targets fixed and measured the pointer. Proteus flips it - the target is programmable. A playing card that can become any other card, on a table where people have been reaching for cards for a few hundred years. The RQ: how much can you change about an object before people stop treating it as the thing they know?</p>
      <p class="label crumb"><a href="/work/scramble/">The Proteus deck →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The favicon turns left</h2>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
          <defs><clipPath id="hx-fav"><path d="M95 50 L72.5 89 L27.5 89 L5 50 L27.5 11 L72.5 11 Z" /></clipPath></defs>
          <rect width="100" height="100" fill="#000" />
          <g clip-path="url(#hx-fav)"><rect x="-60" y="-60" width="220" height="114" fill="#fff" transform="rotate(20 50 50)" /></g>
          <path d="M95 50 L72.5 89 L27.5 89 L5 50 L27.5 11 L72.5 11 Z" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>16 pixels of left bank.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>In the browser tab the instrument is banked 20 degrees left, with the aircraft symbol removed. At 16 pixels, two bars and a dot stop looking like an aircraft and start looking like a face (it did, and it was bad). The left turn isn't random either - circuits at most airports are flown with left turns, so after takeoff, left is the way you go.</p>
      <p>The face is the same problem the Bodhika study is about, at 16 pixels. Shrink something or pull it out of context, and people read it as something else. A wellbeing score on its own reads as a label on the child. The same score inside a whole picture of the child reads as information a parent can act on. Same reason the dashboard got redesigned.</p>
      <p class="label crumb"><a href="/work/bodhika/">The Bodhika dashboards →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="flying">
      <h2>Flying</h2>
      <p>I love flying. Here's some of it on video.</p>
      <ul class="videos">
        {videos.map((v) => (
          <li>
            <a href={v.url} rel="noopener">{v.title}</a>
            <span>{v.note}</span>
            {v.url.startsWith('/') && <video src={v.url} controls preload="metadata" playsinline></video>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      {channel && <p><a href={channel} rel="noopener">All of the videos</a></p>}
      <p class="label crumb"><a href="/projects/">What comes next →</a></p>
    </section>

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Inherits the site's type and rules. Only layout lives here. */
  .attitude { max-width: 40rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.25rem 6rem; }
  .attitude section { border-top: 2px solid #000; padding: 1.75rem 0 3rem; }
  .attitude section h2 { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .attitude section p { margin: 0 0 1.1em; }
  .attitude section p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .attitude .hero { border-top: 0; padding-top: 3rem; text-align: center; }
  .attitude .hero h1 { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }
  .attitude .hero p { margin: 0 auto; max-width: 34rem; }
  .instrument { width: 14rem; height: 14rem; display: block; margin: 0 auto 2rem; }
  .fig { float: right; width: 6.5rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 1.5rem; }
  .fig svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .fig figcaption { font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.3; margin-top: 0.5rem; }
  .attitude section::after { content: ''; display: block; clear: both; }
  .crumb { margin-top: 1.5rem !important; }
  .crumb a { text-decoration: none; }
  .crumb a:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 0.35em; }
  .videos { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .videos li { padding: 0.75rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #000; }
  .videos li:first-child { border-top: 1px solid #000; }
  .videos a { display: block; }
  .videos span { display: block; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .videos video { display: block; width: 100%; margin-top: 0.75rem; background: #000; }
  @media (max-width: 30rem) { .fig { float: none; width: 5rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .ball { transform: translate(0, 18px); }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Reduced motion: stop the SMIL loop and park in the climb.
  if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
    document.querySelectorAll('.instrument animateTransform').forEach((a) => a.remove());
  }
</script>
